On Tue, Sep 21, 2010 at 11:57, Robin Lee Powell <rlpowell@digitalkingdom.org
> wrote:
>
> It's a little bit like the whole "CAhA when unspecified is
> undefined" thing, which is disturbing in that it means that any
> sentence could be false-to-fact without declaring so, but the
> principle of non-gluteality applies better in this case than in that
> one.


Related to this, and to what xalbo said since, is this part of my
fundamental understanding of Lojban: Everything, when unspecified, is
undefined. If you want to define it, specify it. Otherwise, leave it to
context. (And I fully expect that any remotely conversational Lojbanist will
do just that, *often*.)

-- bancus

